[{:seon.task/name "build-smoke-is-green-on-the-pin"
  :seon.task/plan "Jolt's build-smoke.sh fails on our pin 9e42a0d8 at its first check: \"startup profile missing marker: native pre-main (exec+ld)\". A smoke that is red on the pin proves nothing for any Jolt change since. Find whether the marker is missing because of our build (the patched Chez, our launcher) or the test's assumption about its environment, fix it at its cause, and make build-smoke part of what a Jolt pin bump must pass."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-deftype-method-call-takes-a-site-cache"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-27 (task datahike.transaction/an-index-insert-allocates-nothing-extra): an interop call of a deftype's declared method, (.lookup s k cmp), (.cons s k cmp), (.root s) on persistent-sorted-set's PersistentSortedSet, costs 60 ticks and 80 B with no work in it, and a PSS lookup with a free comparator ~300 B. Cause: jolt-host-site-call0..3 (reference-code/jolt/host/chez/java/host-static.ss) cache only a jhost receiver; a jrec builds the argument vector, walks method-dispatch-arms (record-method-dispatch) and the dotform arm's cond (dot-forms.ss), whose rest args become a list, before jrec-method-arity finds the method. The fix: the site keeps (desc . method) for a jrec receiver, as jrec-field-site-ref does for a field read, filled only when the dotform arm would itself take the declared method (no user-override arm, not a field read, not a class field with no args, not an IFn or transient method the arm answers first), and calls it with no vector. Proofs: a Jolt chez test of bytes a call (0 past the method's own), and seon.db-test/a-value-costs-its-index-inserts's overrun closing."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-value-costs-its-index-inserts}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-try-entered-allocates-nothing"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-27 (task datahike.transaction/an-index-insert-allocates-nothing-extra): entering a try/catch whose body does not throw costs 176 B on Jolt ((try (compare a b) (catch Exception e 0)) against 0 B for the compare alone), so a library that wraps a hot call in a try pays it per call; Datahike's compare-value and cmp-attr-nil each grew a type test to skip the try (jolt/compare-value-no-try, jolt/attribute-compare-without-try). The try is emitted at its site as a one-shot escape and a hand-pushed handler (Jolt c22cb9c0, 00a3b321, reference-code/jolt/jolt-core/jolt/backend_scheme.clj); find what of that allocates per entry (the escape continuation, the handler frame, a closure over the body) and make the no-throw path allocate nothing. Once it does, Datahike's two type tests can go. Proof: a Jolt chez test of bytes per try entered (0), and seon.db-test/an-identity-costs-its-seek."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-var-resolved-by-several-fibers-at-once-is-safe"
  :seon.task/plan "Contained 2026-10-27 (jolt 8e5f6304), cause not found. Datahike's background gc hung one run in ten on Jolt: a fiber threw 'car: false is not a pair' and its result channel never answered. The Chez inspector's frames of the failing continuation, innermost first: jolt-stack-check! <- $call-uncounted <- string-hash <- do-hash <- $gen-hashtable-ref <- jolt-var <- var-deref <- do-alts* <- async/merge. So the raise is in the stack-limit hook (rt.ss jolt-stack-check!, installed by sa-gc-install-after-rendezvous!, run after every collection on every thread that took part), which var-deref's allocation only triggered; the earlier trace named var-deref and string-equal? because its frames were abbreviated. Interleaved on one build, 40 rounds each: 5 failures with the hook, 0 with JOLT_STACK_LIMIT=-1. Not the var table (a Chez stress with a locked writer forcing resizes and 7 to 9 unlocked readers, and the same shape in the runtime with 6 threads and 6 fibers, found no error and no lost entry), not short-lived threads starting during collections, and not sa-current-stack-bytes alone (copied into Chez, forced collections at random depths: none). The failure depends on the build (110 runs of builds with debug output: 0; builds without: about 1 in 10) and vanished when the hook's three calls were wrapped, so the faulting car is probably inlined into the hook and layout-dependent; the only unchecked car there is the weak memo's (sa-current-stack-bytes). Done: a failed measurement no longer raises into the computation (it is 'cannot measure', its contract) and is kept, with its frames, for jolt.host/stack-check-failure; proof test/chez/stack-limit-test.ss section 5. Open: read what the kept record says once a run has one (the verification repro, scripts in the root session's notes: bg2.clj prints it), then fix the faulting expression and let the hook's own failure be an error again, as a typed one answered where it lands."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-unchanged-namespace-is-not-emitted-again"
  :seon.task/plan "test-audit item A (2026-10-27, section 5): a one-comment edit to src/seon/profile.clj cost a 48.6 s bin/build seon. Measured (JOLT_BUILD_PROFILE=1, seon.main, 313 units): an unchanged jolt build is 41 s and Chez compiles 1 of 313 units (the per-unit .so cache works, host/chez/build.ss bld-compile-app-units!); the time is the front end over the whole program every build: whole-program inference 11.1 s (fixpoint 8.2 s), emit of every namespace 20.1 s (run-passes 8.7 s, emit-top 8.7 s, dce-refs 2.0 s), write flat.ss 3.8 s (2.0-2.3 s since jolt 6c4f23a0, units written once from memory). Saves cycles, not only wall time (root, 2026-10-27: cycles are the owner's complaint). Before starting: how many builds a day run once the test fixes merge (test-audit's tracking table) decides between this and jolt.backend/whole-program-inference-costs-what-changed. What emit reads and writes (research, 2026-10-27, paths in reference-code/jolt): per namespace, emit (host/chez/emit-image.ss ei-emit-ns-records) takes the IR the whole-program walk made (ei-next-cached; macros already expanded there, build.ss bld-wp-infer!) and reads the whole loaded program: var cells and their final roots (rt.ss var-cell-lookup, var-redefined?; host-contract.ss hc-resolve-cell, hc-var-root-callable?, hc-var-dynamic?, hc-var-linked?), whole-program facts on the unit (jolt-core/jolt/passes/types.clj param-seeds-for, param-num-seeds-for, :field-types, :pm-rets, clone-double-ret?; backend_scheme.clj clone-site?), record shapes and protocol maps (records.ss chez-record-shapes-map, chez-protocol-methods-map), the inline stash (host-contract.ss hc-inline-ir) and the direct-link sets (backend_scheme.clj direct-linkable?, direct-link-fn?). It writes three tables later namespaces and the DCE read: the direct-link sets (backend_scheme.clj emit-def-cached), the inline stash (hc-stash-inline!, IR values) and the spliced set (hc-mark-spliced!, read by dce.ss). The sound design: a per-form cache keyed on a hash of the form's IR (which carries its macro expansion: an expander runs arbitrary code, so its inputs cannot be keyed) and the mode flags, holding the read set recorded at those chokepoints as (fact, digest of its value); a lookup re-reads each recorded fact and hits only when every digest matches (Bazel's and Datahike's read-range validation); a hit replays the three writes, which needs a serializer for IR values (the inline stash), Jolt's first. The name counters (build.ss bld-seed-name-counters!) give each namespace hash(ns) & 0x7FFFF with linear probing in first-call order, so two colliding namespaces name things in build order: the assigned slot goes in the key, or slots are made collision-free first. Estimate: several days; 41 s -> about 17 s on a change that moves no other form's facts. Proofs: the whole program emitted cached and uncached is byte-for-byte equal; a change to a referenced var's facts re-emits its referrers; a cost proof that a one-file change's build is flat across program sizes. An independent review of the key before it merges (root's condition). Not this: JOLT_NO_WP_INFER for dev builds (the binary would allocate unlike main's, and byte cost proofs read it). css stays keyed on src/ (Tailwind scans it for classes)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "emit-runs-in-parallel-processes"
  :seon.task/plan "Lower priority (root, 2026-10-27: it cuts wall time, not cycles). jolt build emits every namespace in one process, in order, 20.1 s for seon.main (2026-10-27), because each namespace's emit reads what earlier ones wrote: the direct-link sets and the inline stash (see jolt.backend/an-unchanged-namespace-is-not-emitted-again). The Chez compile of the units already runs in child processes (build.ss bld-run-jobs-parallel!: 34.3 s sequential, 8.3 s across 8). Correct: compute the direct-link and inline facts from the whole-program walk's IR before emit, then fork children after inference (copy-on-write of the loaded program and its facts) each emitting a slice of the namespaces. Estimate 20 s -> about 5 s wall on every build, the same cycles. Proof: the parallel emit's records equal the sequential emit's byte for byte."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "whole-program-inference-costs-what-changed"
  :seon.task/plan "After jolt.backend/an-unchanged-namespace-is-not-emitted-again, a one-file build is still about 17 s, 11.1 s of it whole-program inference (seon.main, 2026-10-27: wp fixpoint 8.2 s, wp analyze 2.1 s, contagion prepass 0.2 s; host/chez/build.ss bld-wp-infer!). It analyzes every namespace and iterates a global fixpoint from nothing on every build. Correct: cache each namespace's analysis keyed on its text (the emit cache's key discipline: what it reads, recorded), and start the fixpoint from the last build's result, so facts no change reaches converge in one pass and a change re-walks what its facts reach. Estimate about 17 s -> 5 s. Proofs: the fixpoint's result from the last result equals the result from nothing, over the whole program, for a sample of one-namespace edits (a body, a signature, a type hint); a cost proof that inference after a one-file change costs what the change reaches."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"jolt.backend/an-unchanged-namespace-is-not-emitted-again"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-emitted-unit-names-nothing-from-the-jolt-binary"
  :seon.task/plan "Confirmed 2026-10-27 (jolt-compare, building seon.main): any change to Jolt's own image moves the gensym numbers of a few syntax-quote templates in the emitted app units, 18 of 313 (rewrite-clj.node.*, datahike.query.execute, clojure.test.check.random: node__25__auto becomes node__126__auto, and the units' marker offsets with them). Two builds from one Jolt binary are byte-identical, a clean rebuild of the same pin reproduces the old bytes, and a build from a Jolt that differs only in a comment of an embedded runtime file shifts the same 18. Likely cause: those templates are read during the build's load of the app from source (host/chez/build.ss, before the whole-program walk), where the reader's syntax-quote counter is not seeded per namespace (bld-seed-name-counters! seeds the wp and emit phases), so their numbers depend on what the Jolt binary read before. Cost today: none for the unit cache (its key carries the runtime fingerprint, which any Jolt change moves), but a unit's text is not a function of the app and the compiler's version alone, which the emit cache (jolt.backend/an-unchanged-namespace-is-not-emitted-again) and any cross-build comparison need. Correct: seed the reader's counters per namespace for the source load too, or re-read macro templates in the seeded phase. Proof: two Jolt binaries differing only in an embedded comment emit byte-identical units for seon.main."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}]
